      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "### Problem:\nThe first three stages of a pattern are shown below, in which each line segment represents a toothpick. If the pattern continues such that each successive stage has three more toothpicks than the previous one, how many toothpicks are there in the arrangement for the 250th stage?\n  \n[asy]\nsize(150);\ndefaultpen(linewidth(0.7));\nvoid drawSquare(pair A){\n\n  draw((A.x + 0.1, A.y)--(A.x + 0.9, A.y));\n\n  draw((A.x, A.y + 0.1)--(A.x, A.y + 0.9));\n\n  draw((A.x + 1, A.y + 0.1)--(A.x + 1, A.y + 0.9));\n\n  draw((A.x + 0.1, A.y + 1)--(A.x + 0.9, A.y + 1));\n}\n\nint k = 0;\n\nfor(int i = 1; i <= 3; ++ i){\n\n  for(int j = 0; j < i; ++j){\n\n    drawSquare((k,0));\n\n    ++k;\n\n  }\n\n  draw((k + 0.1, 0.5)--(k + 0.9, 0.5), EndArrow);\n\n  ++k;\n}\nlabel(\"$\\cdots$\", (k, 0.5));\n[/asy]\n\nWe can solve the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Identify the number of toothpicks in the first three stages:**\n   - First stage: 4 toothpicks.\n   - Second stage: $4 + 3 = 7$ toothpicks.\n   - Third stage: $7 + 3 = 10$ toothpicks.\n\n2. **Determine the pattern:**\n   The pattern is that each stage has three more toothpicks than the previous one. This means the number of toothpicks in each stage forms an arithmetic sequence where the first term is 4 and the common difference is 3.\n\n3. **Find the number of toothpicks in the \\( n \\)-th stage:**\n   The number of toothpicks in the \\( n \\)-th stage is given by the formula for the \\( n \\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence:\n   \\[\n   a_n = a_1 + (n-1) \\cdot d\n   \\]\n   where \\( a_1 = 4 \\) and \\( d = 3 \\).\n\n4. **Calculate the number of toothpicks for the 250th stage:**\n   \\[\n   a_{250} = 4 + (250-1) \\cdot 3 = 4 + 249 \\cdot 3 = 4 + 747 = 751\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of toothpicks necessary to create the arrangement for the 250th stage is \\(\\boxed{751}\\).",
